          <SocialProof users="8.4k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#do-i-need-to" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Do You Actually Need To?</a>
          <a href="#methods" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Removal Methods Compared</a>
          <a href="#process" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Removal Process</a>
          <a href="#why-buildup" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Why Buildup Happens</a>
          <a href="#when-to-stop" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. When Removal Is Not the Answer</a>
          <a href="#tools" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Tools and Safety</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Sealer that is lifting in plates is not worn out — it is over-applied. Recoating it hides the problem for one season and makes the eventual removal job substantially worse, because you are adding another layer to a stack that already failed under its own rigidity.
        </p>

        <div id="do-i-need-to" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Do You Actually Need to Remove It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Removal is hard work and it is not always necessary. Most driveways due for resealing need cleaning, not stripping. The distinction is between sealer that has worn and sealer that has failed.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What You See</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Diagnosis</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Action</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Even fading to grey in traffic lanes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Normal wear</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean and recoat; no removal needed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Aggregate showing through thin areas</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Normal wear</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clean and recoat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Black flakes lifting in plates</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Buildup failure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove the loose material</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fine web cracking in the sealer only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Buildup, too rigid to flex</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove affected areas</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Peeling around an oil stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bond failure, localised</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove locally, degrease, prime</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tacky or soft years after application</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Never cured properly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove; it will not improve</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The test is simple. Push a stiff putty knife or scraper under an edge. If the sealer lifts away in a piece thick enough to hold its shape, you have buildup and that material needs to come off. If the surface is simply thin and grey and the scraper finds nothing to lift, it has worn normally and a wash is all the preparation it needs.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Partial Removal Is Usually Enough</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">You rarely need to strip a driveway back to bare asphalt.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The goal is to remove everything loose, flaking or unsound, and to feather the edges of what remains so the new coat does not telegraph a hard line. Sound, well-bonded sealer can stay. Chasing a completely bare surface turns a weekend job into a project and risks damaging the asphalt.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="methods" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Three Removal Methods Compared
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Method</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Effort</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Risk to Asphalt</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Scraping</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Loose flakes and plates</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High, but controllable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low if the blade is kept flat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pressure washing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large areas of failing sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Real — can strip aggregate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chemical stripper</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stubborn, well-bonded buildup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate, plus disposal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies; can soften binder</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Scraping</h3>

          <p>
            The most controlled method and the right starting point for almost every driveway. A long-handled floor scraper or a sturdy putty knife lifts loose plates efficiently, and because you are working mechanically at a specific spot you can feel when you have reached sound material and stop. Keep the blade close to flat against the surface — a steep angle digs into asphalt rather than under sealer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pressure Washing</h3>

          <p>
            Effective across large areas of genuinely failing sealer, and it doubles as the cleaning step. The risk is the same one that applies to any pressure washing on asphalt: enough pressure to lift bonded sealer is approaching enough pressure to cut binder and blast aggregate loose. Work at moderate pressure with a wide fan, keep the wand moving, and accept that stubborn well-bonded areas should be left rather than escalated.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Chemical Strippers</h3>

          <p>
            A last resort for buildup that will not yield to scraping or washing. Products vary widely and some are aggressive enough to soften the asphalt binder as well as the sealer, which trades one problem for a worse one. If you use one, test a small area first, follow the dwell times precisely, and plan for the runoff — stripper plus dissolved sealer is not something to rinse into a storm drain. Check local disposal rules before you start.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="process" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Working Through the Removal
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Sweep and assess the whole driveway</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Map where the failure actually is. Buildup is often concentrated in areas that got extra product, typically near the garage where people start.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Scrape the loose material first</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Work with a long-handled scraper held close to flat. Take off everything that lifts willingly and stop where the material resists.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Pressure wash the scraped areas</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Moderate pressure, wide fan tip, wand always moving. This lifts partially bonded material the scraper left and cleans the surface at the same time.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Feather the edges of remaining sealer</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Where sound sealer meets bare asphalt, work the boundary so it tapers rather than steps. A hard edge telegraphs through the new coat.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">5</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Treat any oil contamination now</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Peeling around a stain means the bond failed there. Degrease, scrub, and use an oil spot primer, or the new coat will fail in the same place.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">6</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Collect and dispose of debris properly</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Sealer flakes are not garden waste. Bag them and check local guidance on disposal rather than washing them into a drain.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">7</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Let the surface dry completely</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A full 24 dry hours after washing, longer if cool or humid. Check shaded areas and cracks, which hold moisture longest.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">8</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Fill cracks, then recoat</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">With the surface sound and dry, fill any cracks, let the filler cure, then apply two thin coats of sealer.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Expect the finished driveway to look somewhat variable where old sealer remains alongside newly bared asphalt. Two coats of fresh sealer evens this out considerably, but a driveway that has had buildup removed will rarely look as uniform as one that never had the problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-buildup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Sealer Builds Up in the First Place
          </h2>

          <p>
            Understanding the cause matters, because removal without changing the habit means doing this again in a few years.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sealing Too Often</h3>

          <p>
            The most common cause. Each coat applied before the previous one has worn thin stacks fresh material onto an intact film. The total thickness climbs with every application until the combined layer is too rigid to flex with the asphalt underneath, at which point it cracks in a fine web and lifts in plates. A driveway sealed every spring for six years is usually in worse condition than one sealed twice in that period.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Applying Too Heavily</h3>

          <p>
            One thick coat instead of two thin ones produces a film that skins over on top while remaining wet underneath. It never cures properly through its full depth, stays comparatively soft and brittle, and fails early. Sealcoat is designed to go on thin, at roughly a gallon per 80 to 100 square feet per coat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sealing Over a Poor Bond</h3>

          <p>
            Sealer applied over dust, over a damp surface or over untreated oil never anchored properly in the first place. It may look fine for a season, then lift in exactly the areas where the bond was worst. This failure looks like buildup but the cause is preparation.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Habit That Prevents a Repeat</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Reseal when it has worn grey, not on a schedule.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Look at the driveway rather than the calendar. Traffic lanes faded to consistent grey, aggregate becoming visible, and water soaking in rather than beading all mean the film has genuinely worn thin and it is time. That is typically every two to three years for asphalt emulsion, but the surface decides, not the anniversary.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-stop" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When the Driveway Needs More Than a Strip and Reseal
          </h2>

          <p>
            Removing sealer is worth doing when there is a sound driveway underneath worth recoating. Sometimes there is not, and it is better to establish that before spending a weekend scraping.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>If removing the sealer reveals widespread alligator cracking, the base has failed and resealing will not address it.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>If large areas of aggregate come away with the sealer, the asphalt itself has ravelled beyond what a coating restores.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>If sections flex or rebound under a vehicle, there is no support beneath and the pavement needs rebuilding.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>If the asphalt has worn so thin that base material is visible in places, resurfacing is the honest answer.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            In those cases the money is better spent on resurfacing — a new layer of asphalt over the existing surface — or on full replacement. Sealer applied to a failed pavement is a cosmetic treatment on a structural problem, and it will be removed along with everything else when the driveway is eventually rebuilt.
          </p>

          <p>
            The encouraging version of this is more common though. Most driveways with peeling sealer have perfectly sound asphalt underneath, and a weekend of scraping followed by proper preparation and two thin coats produces a surface that looks and performs better than it has in years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Tools, Disposal and Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Removal is more physical and messier than sealing, and a few things are worth having sorted before you start rather than discovering mid-job.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A long-handled floor scraper with a replaceable blade, which saves your back over a stiff putty knife.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Spare blades — scraping cured sealer dulls an edge faster than most people expect.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A stiff push broom and a shovel for collecting flakes, which are surprisingly voluminous.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Heavy contractor bags; sealer debris is heavy and tears light bags.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Eye protection, particularly when pressure washing, as flakes come off at speed.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Gloves and long sleeves, especially if using any chemical stripper.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>A tarp or sheeting to protect adjacent lawn, beds and paving from debris and runoff.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Disposal Is Not an Afterthought</h3>

          <p>
            Removed sealer flakes are not garden waste and should not be swept into a storm drain, a gutter or a garden bed. Coal tar based products in particular carry environmental restrictions in a number of states and municipalities specifically because of runoff concerns. Bag the debris and check your local authority’s guidance on disposal before the job rather than after.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same applies with more force to chemical stripping. Stripper mixed with dissolved sealer is a genuinely problematic liquid, and rinsing it into a drain is both an environmental issue and, in many places, a regulatory one. If you cannot contain and dispose of that runoff properly, scraping and washing are the better methods regardless of how stubborn the buildup is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pace Yourself</h3>

          <p>
            Scraping a full driveway is genuinely hard work, harder than applying sealer, and there is no benefit to completing it in one session. Working across two mornings while the weather holds is easier on your back and produces better results, because fatigue is what leads to steep scraper angles and gouged asphalt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating Straight Over Peeling Sealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new coat over flaking material bonds to the flakes, not to the driveway, so the whole assembly lifts together — and now there is one more layer on a stack that already failed under its own rigidity. It buys one season and makes the eventual removal substantially harder.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Holding the Scraper at Too Steep an Angle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A blade held steeply digs into the asphalt rather than sliding under the sealer, gouging the surface you are trying to preserve. Keep the scraper close to flat and let the edge work under the failing material. If it resists at a flat angle, that section is probably still well bonded and can stay.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Escalating Pressure Until Something Gives</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When stubborn sealer will not lift, the instinct is to switch to a narrower tip or move closer. That is the point at which pressure washing starts cutting the petroleum binder and blasting aggregate loose, causing permanent damage. Leave well-bonded areas in place and feather the edges instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Chemical Stripper Without Testing First</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some strippers are aggressive enough to soften the asphalt binder along with the sealer, trading a coating problem for a pavement problem. Always test a small inconspicuous area, follow the dwell time exactly, and plan disposal in advance — stripper and dissolved sealer must not go into a storm drain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Removing the Sealer and Then Sealing Too Often Again</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Buildup is caused by resealing before the previous coat has worn thin. Strip a driveway, then return to an annual sealing habit, and the same failure returns within a few years. Reseal when the traffic lanes have gone grey and water soaks in rather than beading — typically every two to three years.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you remove old driveway sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start by scraping loose flakes and plates with a long-handled scraper held close to flat, then pressure wash the scraped areas at moderate pressure with a wide fan tip. Chemical strippers are a last resort for stubborn buildup. You rarely need to strip back to bare asphalt — removing everything loose and feathering the edges is usually enough.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I have to remove old sealer before resealing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it is failing. Sealer that has simply worn thin and faded to grey needs cleaning, not removal. Sealer that lifts in plates thick enough to hold their shape, cracks in a fine web, or is still tacky years after application is buildup and must come off before recoating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my driveway sealer peeling off in sheets?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always buildup from over-application — either sealing too often, before the previous coat had worn thin, or applying one thick coat instead of two thin ones. The accumulated film becomes too rigid to flex with the asphalt underneath and cracks, then water gets beneath it and freeze-thaw cycling lifts it in plates.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you pressure wash driveway sealer off?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes for genuinely failing sealer, with care. The pressure needed to lift bonded sealer approaches the pressure that cuts asphalt binder and blasts aggregate loose. Use moderate pressure with a wide fan tip, keep the wand moving, and leave well-bonded areas in place rather than escalating to a narrower tip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What causes driveway sealer to build up?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Resealing before the previous coat has worn thin is the main cause, since each application stacks fresh material on an intact film until the combined thickness cannot flex with the pavement. Applying one heavy coat rather than two thin ones, and sealing over dust or damp, contribute the same way.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth stripping a driveway with badly peeling sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually yes, provided the asphalt underneath is sound. Most driveways with peeling sealer have perfectly good pavement below it. If removal reveals widespread alligator cracking, loose aggregate across large areas or sections that flex under a vehicle, the driveway needs resurfacing rather than recoating.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

