        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            First decide whether you need to remove anything at all. Sealer that has faded evenly to grey has worn normally and needs a wash, not a strip. Sealer that lifts in plates thick enough to hold their shape is buildup, and coating over it only adds to the stack that already failed.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            When it does need to come off, scrape first with the blade close to flat, then pressure wash at moderate pressure with a wide fan. Take off everything loose, feather the boundary where sound sealer remains, and resist the urge to escalate pressure on stubborn areas — that is where asphalt damage happens. Complete bare-asphalt removal is rarely necessary.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then change the habit that caused it. Buildup comes from resealing before the last coat wore thin, or from applying one heavy coat instead of two thin ones. Let the driveway tell you when it is due — grey traffic lanes and water soaking in rather than beading — and apply two thin coats when that day comes. Typically that is every two to three years, not every spring.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
